===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[infatuated|Infatuated]], foolishly attracted to (someone).
#* '''1901''', {{w|J. M. Barrie}}, ''{{w|Quality Street (play)|Quality Street}}'', Act III,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/31266/31266-h/31266-h.htm]</sup>
#*: He is '''infatuate''' about her.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[foolish|Foolish]], lacking good [[judgement]].
#* {{c.|1521}} {{w|John Skelton}}, “Speke Parott”:
#*: Helas I lamente the dull abuſyd brayne<br>The '''enfatuate''' fantaſies the wytles wylfulnes<br>Of on and hothyr at me that haue dyſdayne
#* '''1623''', {{w|Joseph Hall (bishop)|Joseph Hall}}, ''Contemplations on the Historical Passages of the Old and New Testaments'' (original title ''Contemplations vpon the Historie of the Old Testament''), Edinburgh: Ja. Robertson ''et al.'', 1796, Volume 2, Book 18, Contemplation 4, p. 167,<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/008901164]</sup>
#*: There was never wicked man that was not '''infatuate''', and in nothing more than in those things wherein he hoped most to transcend the reach of others.
#* '''1918''', {{w|George Bernard Shaw}}, “A Letter to {{w|Frank Harris}}, published by him in his ''Life of Wilde''” in ''Pen Portraits and Reviews'', London: Constable, 1931, p. 293,<sup>[http://www.fadedpage.com/books/20141087/html.php]</sup>
#*: {{w|Oscar Wilde|Wilde}} was in a curious double temper. He made no pretence either of innocence or of questioning the folly of his proceedings against {{w|John Douglas, 9th Marquess of Queensberry|Queensberry}}. But he had an '''infatuate''' haughtiness as to the impossibility of his retreating, and as to his right to dictate your course.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[infatuated|Infatuated]] person.
#* '''1771''', {{w|Elizabeth Griffith}}, ''The History of Lady Barton'', London: T. Davies & T. Cadell, Volume I, Letter 26, p. 183,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004844214.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} she has a number of relations here, brothers and cousins, by the dozen; but they are all priests, and I am apprehensive that some of these '''infatuates''' may persuade her to quit me, and lock her up in a convent {{...}}
#* '''1911''', {{w|Max Beerbohm}}, ''{{w|Zuleika Dobson}}'', New York: John Lane, Chapter 4, p. 59,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/ost-english-zuleikadobsonbee00beer]</sup>
#*: The idol has come sliding down its pedestal to fawn and grovel with all the other '''infatuates''' in the dust about my feet.

